<article class="ltx_document ltx_authors_1line" lang="en">
<h1 class="ltx_title ltx_title_document">Training Task Reasoning LLM Agents for Multi-turn Task Planning via Single-turn Reinforcement Learning<span class="ltx_pubnotes"><span class="ltx_pubnotes_content"><span class="ltx_pubnote ltx_role_thanks"><span class="ltx_note_name">Thanks: </span>
This work was done while Hanjiang Hu was a research intern and Na Li was a visiting scholar at Mitsubishi Electric Research Laboratories (MERL), Cambridge, MA 02139, USA. (email: <span id="id1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter" style="font-size:90%;">hanjianghu@cmu.edu</span>). Yebin Wang is with the MERL. (email: <span id="id2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter" style="font-size:90%;">yebinwang@ieee.org).</span></span></span></span></h1>
<div class="ltx_authors">
<span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Hanjiang Hu   Changliu Liu   Na Li   Yebin Wang
</span><span class="ltx_author_notes"><span class="ltx_author_notes_content">
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_affiliation"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Affiliation: </span>Carnegie Mellon University  
</span>
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_affiliation"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Affiliation: </span>Harvard University
</span>
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_affiliation"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Affiliation: </span>Mitsubishi Electric Research Laboratories (MERL)

</span></span></span></span></div>

<div id="abstract1" class="ltx_abstract"><h6 class="ltx_title ltx_title_abstract">Abstract</h6>
    
<p id="abstract1.1" class="ltx_p">Large Language Models (LLMs) have demonstrated remarkable capabilities in knowledge acquisition, reasoning, and tool use, making them promising candidates for autonomous agent applications. However, training LLM agents for complex multi-turn task planning faces significant challenges, including sparse episode-wise rewards, credit assignment across long horizons, and the computational overhead of reinforcement learning in multi-turn interaction settings. To this end, this paper introduces a novel approach that transforms multi-turn task planning into single-turn task reasoning problems, enabling efficient policy optimization through Group Relative Policy Optimization (GRPO) with dense and verifiable reward from expert trajectories. Our theoretical analysis shows that GRPO improvement on single-turn task reasoning results in higher multi-turn success probability under the minimal turns, as well as the generalization to subtasks with shorter horizons. Experimental evaluation on the complex task planning benchmark demonstrates that our 1.5B parameter model trained with single-turn GRPO achieves superior performance compared to larger baseline models up to 14B parameters, with success rates of 70% for long-horizon planning tasks with over 30 steps. We also theoretically and empirically validate the strong cross-task generalizability that the models trained on complex tasks can lead to the successful completion of all simpler subtasks.</p>
  
</div>
<section id="S1" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">1 </span>Introduction</h2>

<div id="S1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Large Language Models (LLMs) as autonomous agents are important in modern AI-based systems, which can perceive environments, reason about plans, and execute actions to interact with the environments <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">1</a>]</cite>.
Modern LLM agents demonstrate strong capabilities in knowledge integration, multi-step reasoning, and adaptive planning, as evidenced by their success in applications ranging from web search to robotic control <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">2</a>, <a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">3</a>]</cite>. On top of these capabilities, prompt-based agentic frameworks <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">4</a>, <a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">5</a>, <a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">6</a>]</cite> are proposed by integrating observation for environment state, reasoning based on augmented LLM with tools and memory, and action execution that interacts with the environment through structured interfaces as a series of <span id="S1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">single-turn</span> interactions with the environments. However, effort-costly prompt engineering is inevitable to build the LLM-based agent, and it is also computationally expensive for test-time scaling in the <span id="S1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">multi-turn</span> interaction with the environment <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">7</a>, <a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">8</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Therefore, training LLM agents through reinforcement learning (RL) for complex multi-turn task planning becomes a promising way to build effective agentic systems with low test-time cost <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">9</a>, <a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">10</a>, <a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">11</a>]</cite>. However, current RL approaches face critical challenges when applied to multi-turn interactions
with the environment for LLMs <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">12</a>, <a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">13</a>, <a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">14</a>, <a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">15</a>]</cite>. The reward of task completion is too sparse for effective exploitation of LLM policy in the multi-turn settings. Besides, the credit assignment becomes particularly difficult in long-horizon planning problems, where determining which specific actions contribute to the eventual reward of success or failure is non-trivial <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">10</a>]</cite>. The computational complexity of multi-turn RL scales poorly with sequence length, making it impractical for training on complex tasks that may require dozens of sequential decision turns <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">16</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">In contrast, recent RL post-training successfully boosts LLM reasoning capability, particularly in math and coding domains based on on-policy methods, e.g., Group Relative Policy Optimization (GRPO) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">17</a>, <a href="#bib.bib18" title="" class="ltx_ref">18</a>]</cite>. However, these successes have primarily focused on single-turn reasoning tasks
with verifiable reward, where the model generates a complete solution in one step and receives the reward directly based on the correctness of the final answer <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib19" title="" class="ltx_ref">19</a>]</cite>. The fundamental challenge lies in bridging the gap between single-turn reasoning capabilities and multi-turn task planning requirements, where LLM agents must be coherent along the interaction sequences while adapting to changing environmental states.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">To this end, we aim to bridge the gap based on the insight that complex multi-turn task planning can be decomposed into a sequence of single-turn task reasoning problems, each requiring the agent to select the optimal next action given the current state.
This decomposition enables us to leverage the provably effective and efficient RL post-training with dense and verifiable reward based on expert trajectory, with theoretical guarantees of the multi-turn decision-making capabilities for long-horizon planning. We compare and validate the effectiveness and generalizability of the proposed method on the challenging long-horizon task planning benchmark. The contributions are summarized below.</p>
<ul id="S1.I1" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="S1.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We provide a theoretical analysis bridging single-turn task reasoning and multi-turn task completion, proving that single-turn GRPO improves the success probability in multi-turn planning scenarios.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We show empirical results on multiple tasks from the challenging <span id="S1.I1.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Robotouille</span> benchmark, where our 1.5B model trained with single-turn GRPO consistently outperforms 14B baselines with fewer completion steps.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We both theoretically and empirically show the strong cross-task generalization capabilities of the GRPO-optimized LLM agents, where models trained on complex tasks successfully transfer to simpler subtasks.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">2 </span>Problem Formulation</h2>

<div id="S2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We formulate the multi-turn task planning problem as two interconnected Markov Decision Processes (MDPs): a multi-turn MDP representing the complete long-horizon task planning and a single-turn MDP based on expert trajectories for training purposes.</p>
</div>
<section id="S2.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">2.1 </span>Multi-Turn Task Planning MDP</h3>

<div id="S2.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}=(\mathcal{S},\mathcal{A},f,R,\mathcal{T},s_{0})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mo>,</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>f</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>R</mi><mo>,</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}=(\mathcal{S},\mathcal{A},f,R,\mathcal{T},s_{0})</annotation></semantics></math> be a finite-horizon MDP representing the multi-turn task planning problem, where <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{S}</annotation></semantics></math> is the token state space representing the current task context and environment observation; <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{A}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{A}</annotation></semantics></math> is the token action space of discrete possible single-step actions; <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f:\mathcal{S}\times\mathcal{A}\rightarrow\mathcal{S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f:\mathcal{S}\times\mathcal{A}\rightarrow\mathcal{S}</annotation></semantics></math> is the deterministic but unknown token state transition function; <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R:\mathcal{S}\times\mathcal{A}\rightarrow\{0,1\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R:\mathcal{S}\times\mathcal{A}\rightarrow\{0,1\}</annotation></semantics></math> is the binary sparse reward function indicating whether the task is completed; <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{T}\subset\mathbb{N}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><mo>⊂</mo><mi>ℕ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{T}\subset\mathbb{N}</annotation></semantics></math> is the finite time horizon; <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s_{0}\in\mathcal{S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s_{0}\in\mathcal{S}</annotation></semantics></math> is the initial state.
Let <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi:\mathcal{S}\rightarrow\mathcal{A}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>π</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi:\mathcal{S}\rightarrow\mathcal{A}</annotation></semantics></math> be a stochastic policy mapping token states to token action distributions. <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi(a|s)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>π</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi(a|s)</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the probability of <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a=\pi(s)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>π</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a=\pi(s)</annotation></semantics></math>. A trajectory <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau=[(s_{0},a_{0}),(s_{1},a_{1}),\ldots,(s_{T},a_{T})]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau=[(s_{0},a_{0}),(s_{1},a_{1}),\ldots,(s_{T},a_{T})]</annotation></semantics></math> is generated by following policy <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>π</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi</annotation></semantics></math> for <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math> steps, where <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s_{i+1}=f(s_{i},a_{i})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s_{i+1}=f(s_{i},a_{i})</annotation></semantics></math>. Then we define the successful trajectory induced by a stochastic policy as follows.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.Thmdefinition1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_definition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S2.Thmdefinition1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Definition 2.1</span></span><span id="S2.Thmdefinition1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Successful trajectory by stochastic policy)<span id="S2.Thmdefinition1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S2.Thmdefinition1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.Thmdefinition1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.Thmdefinition1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Given the MDP <math id="S2.Thmdefinition1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}=(\mathcal{S},\mathcal{A},f,R,\mathcal{T},s_{0})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mo>,</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>f</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>R</mi><mo>,</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}=(\mathcal{S},\mathcal{A},f,R,\mathcal{T},s_{0})</annotation></semantics></math>, a successful trajectory starting with <math id="S2.Thmdefinition1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s_{0}^{s}=s_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>s</mi><mn>0</mn><mi>s</mi></msubsup><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s_{0}^{s}=s_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> is defined as:</span></p>
<table id="S2.EGx1" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S2.E1"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.E1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\tau^{s}(T)=[(s_{0}^{s},a_{0}^{s}),\ldots,(s_{T}^{s},a_{T}^{s})],\ R(s_{T}^{s},a_{T}^{s})=1," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>τ</mi><mi>s</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>s</mi><mn>0</mn><mi>s</mi></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>a</mi><mn>0</mn><mi>s</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>s</mi><mi>T</mi><mi>s</mi></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>a</mi><mi>T</mi><mi>s</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.667em">,</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>s</mi><mi>T</mi><mi>s</mi></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>a</mi><mi>T</mi><mi>s</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\tau^{s}(T)=[(s_{0}^{s},a_{0}^{s}),\ldots,(s_{T}^{s},a_{T}^{s})],\ R(s_{T}^{s},a_{T}^{s})=1,</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(1)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.Thmdefinition1.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.Thmdefinition1.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">where <math id="S2.Thmdefinition1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s_{i+1}^{s}=f(s_{i}^{s},a_{i}^{s})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>s</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>s</mi></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>s</mi><mi>i</mi><mi>s</mi></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>a</mi><mi>i</mi><mi>s</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s_{i+1}^{s}=f(s_{i}^{s},a_{i}^{s})</annotation></semantics></math> for <math id="S2.Thmdefinition1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i=0,1,\dots,T-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i=0,1,\dots,T-1</annotation></semantics></math>; <math id="S2.Thmdefinition1.p1.m5" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="a_{i}^{s}\sim\pi(\cdot|s_{i}^{s})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>a</mi><mi>i</mi><mi>s</mi></msubsup><mo>∼</mo><mi>π</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><msubsup><mi>s</mi><mi>i</mi><mi>s</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a_{i}^{s}\sim\pi(\cdot|s_{i}^{s})</annotation></semantics></math> for <math id="S2.Thmdefinition1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i=0,1,2,\dots,T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i=0,1,2,\dots,T</annotation></semantics></math> given the stochastic policy <math id="S2.Thmdefinition1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>π</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">2.2 </span>Expert Trajectory with Unique Optimality</h3>

<div id="S2.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">On top of <a href="#S2.Thmdefinition1" title="Definition 2.1 (Successful trajectory by stochastic policy). ‣ 2.1 Multi-Turn Task Planning MDP ‣ 2 Problem Formulation ‣ Training Task Reasoning LLM Agents for Multi-turn Task Planning via Single-turn Reinforcement Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Definition</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.1</span></a>, we further define the expert trajectory generated by the expert policy with the unique optimality of minimal trajectory length.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.Thmdefinition2" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_definition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S2.Thmdefinition2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Definition 2.2</span></span><span id="S2.Thmdefinition2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Expert trajectory by stochastic policy)<span id="S2.Thmdefinition2.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S2.Thmdefinition2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.Thmdefinition2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.Thmdefinition2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Given the MDP <math id="S2.Thmdefinition2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}=(\mathcal{S},\mathcal{A},f,R,\mathcal{T},s_{0})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mo>,</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>f</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>R</mi><mo>,</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}=(\mathcal{S},\mathcal{A},f,R,\mathcal{T},s_{0})</annotation></semantics></math>, a trajectory <math id="S2.Thmdefinition2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau^{GT}(T^{GT})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>τ</mi><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>T</mi><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau^{GT}(T^{GT})</annotation></semantics></math> from the stochastic policy <math id="S2.Thmdefinition2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>π</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi</annotation></semantics></math> is defined as an expert trajectory if the following conditions hold:</span></p>
<ol id="S2.I1" class="ltx_enumerate">
<li id="S2.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">1.</span> 
<div id="S2.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S2.I1.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau^{GT}(T^{GT})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>τ</mi><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>T</mi><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau^{GT}(T^{GT})</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S2.I1.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> is a successful trajectory generated from stochastic policy the </span><math id="S2.I1.i1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>π</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S2.I1.i1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> in </span><a href="#S2.Thmdefinition1" title="Definition 2.1 (Successful trajectory by stochastic policy). ‣ 2.1 Multi-Turn Task Planning MDP ‣ 2 Problem Formulation ‣ Training Task Reasoning LLM Agents for Multi-turn Task Planning via Single-turn Reinforcement Learning" class="ltx_ref ltx_font_italic"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Definition</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.1</span></a><span id="S2.I1.i1.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">.</span></p>
</div></li>
<li id="S2.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">2.</span> 
<div id="S2.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.I1.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">For any successful trajectory </span><math id="S2.I1.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau(T)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau(T)</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S2.I1.i2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> generated by stochastic policy </span><math id="S2.I1.i2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>π</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S2.I1.i2.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> with </span><math id="S2.I1.i2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R(s_{T},a_{T})=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R(s_{T},a_{T})=1</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S2.I1.i2.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">, we have </span><math id="S2.I1.i2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T\geq T^{GT}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>≥</mo><msup><mi>T</mi><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T\geq T^{GT}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S2.I1.i2.p1.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> and the equality holds if and only if </span><math id="S2.I1.i2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau=\tau^{GT}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>τ</mi><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau=\tau^{GT}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S2.I1.i2.p1.1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">.</span></p>
</div></li>
</ol>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Based on the expert trajectory <math id="S2.SS2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau^{GT}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>τ</mi><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau^{GT}</annotation></semantics></math> in <a href="#S2.Thmdefinition2" title="Definition 2.2 (Expert trajectory by stochastic policy). ‣ 2.2 Expert Trajectory with Unique Optimality ‣ 2 Problem Formulation ‣ Training Task Reasoning LLM Agents for Multi-turn Task Planning via Single-turn Reinforcement Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Definition</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.2</span></a>, we can further define the expert policy <math id="S2.SS2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi^{GT}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>π</mi><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi^{GT}</annotation></semantics></math> as follows.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.Thmdefinition3" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_definition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S2.Thmdefinition3.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Definition 2.3</span></span><span id="S2.Thmdefinition3.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Expert policy from expert trajectory)<span id="S2.Thmdefinition3.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S2.Thmdefinition3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.Thmdefinition3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.Thmdefinition3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Given the MDP <math id="S2.Thmdefinition3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}=(\mathcal{S},\mathcal{A},f,R,\mathcal{T},s_{0})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mo>,</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>f</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>R</mi><mo>,</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}=(\mathcal{S},\mathcal{A},f,R,\mathcal{T},s_{0})</annotation></semantics></math> and an expert trajectory <math id="S2.Thmdefinition3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau^{GT}(T^{GT})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>τ</mi><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>T</mi><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau^{GT}(T^{GT})</annotation></semantics></math>, a policy <math id="S2.Thmdefinition3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi^{GT}:\mathcal{S}\rightarrow\mathcal{A}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>π</mi><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi^{GT}:\mathcal{S}\rightarrow\mathcal{A}</annotation></semantics></math> is defined as the expert policy if it holds that</span></p>
<table id="S2.EGx2" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S2.E2"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.E2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle a_{i}^{GT}=\pi^{GT}(s_{i}^{GT}),\ \forall(s_{i}^{GT},a_{i}^{GT})\in\tau^{GT}(T^{GT})." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>a</mi><mi>i</mi><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mi>π</mi><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>s</mi><mi>i</mi><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.667em">,</mo><mrow><mrow><mo>∀</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>s</mi><mi>i</mi><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>a</mi><mi>i</mi><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msup><mi>τ</mi><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>T</mi><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle a_{i}^{GT}=\pi^{GT}(s_{i}^{GT}),\ \forall(s_{i}^{GT},a_{i}^{GT})\in\tau^{GT}(T^{GT}).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(2)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.Thmremark1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_remark">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S2.Thmremark1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Remark 2.1</span></span><span id="S2.Thmremark1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S2.Thmremark1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.Thmremark1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.Thmremark1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Even though expert trajectory <math id="S2.Thmremark1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau^{GT}(T^{GT})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>τ</mi><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>T</mi><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau^{GT}(T^{GT})</annotation></semantics></math> is uniquely optimal with minimal length of turns <math id="S2.Thmremark1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T^{GT}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>T</mi><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T^{GT}</annotation></semantics></math>, the induced expert policy <math id="S2.Thmremark1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi^{GT}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>π</mi><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi^{GT}</annotation></semantics></math> is not necessarily unique or deterministic, as there is no state-action
constraint beyond expert trajectories <math id="S2.Thmremark1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau^{GT}(T^{GT})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>τ</mi><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>T</mi><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau^{GT}(T^{GT})</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">2.3 </span>Single-Turn Task Reasoning MDP</h3>

<div id="S2.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Even though multi-turn task planning MDP <math id="S2.SS3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}</annotation></semantics></math> is the conventional one in the control and RL community, it is challenging and non-trivial to directly train an LLM policy for <math id="S2.SS3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}</annotation></semantics></math> due to intractable computational overhead with exponentially increasing token length of trajectory rollouts in the multi-turn setting <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">16</a>, <a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">10</a>]</cite>. Therefore, to enable efficient LLM policy learning
, we construct an additional single-turn MDP <math id="S2.SS3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}_{S}=(\mathcal{S},\mathcal{A},\emptyset,r_{\pi^{GT}},\mathit{1},s_{0})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mi>S</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mo>,</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∅</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><msup><mi>π</mi><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow></msup></msub><mo>,</mo><mn class="ltx_mathvariant_italic" mathvariant="italic">1</mn><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}_{S}=(\mathcal{S},\mathcal{A},\emptyset,r_{\pi^{GT}},\mathit{1},s_{0})</annotation></semantics></math> based on the expert trajectory <math id="S2.SS3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau^{GT}(T^{GT})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>τ</mi><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>T</mi><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau^{GT}(T^{GT})</annotation></semantics></math> in <a href="#S2.Thmdefinition2" title="Definition 2.2 (Expert trajectory by stochastic policy). ‣ 2.2 Expert Trajectory with Unique Optimality ‣ 2 Problem Formulation ‣ Training Task Reasoning LLM Agents for Multi-turn Task Planning via Single-turn Reinforcement Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Definition</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.2</span></a> and expert policy <math id="S2.SS3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi^{GT}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>π</mi><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi^{GT}</annotation></semantics></math> in <a href="#S2.Thmdefinition3" title="Definition 2.3 (Expert policy from expert trajectory). ‣ 2.2 Expert Trajectory with Unique Optimality ‣ 2 Problem Formulation ‣ Training Task Reasoning LLM Agents for Multi-turn Task Planning via Single-turn Reinforcement Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Definition</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.3</span></a>. This single-turn MDP can be viewed as a bandit problem as follows.
<math id="S2.SS3.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{S}</annotation></semantics></math> is the same token state space as in <math id="S2.SS3.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S2.SS3.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{A}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{A}</annotation></semantics></math> is the same token action space as in <math id="S2.SS3.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S2.SS3.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r_{\pi^{GT}}:\mathcal{S}\times\mathcal{A}\rightarrow\{0,1\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><msup><mi>π</mi><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow></msup></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r_{\pi^{GT}}:\mathcal{S}\times\mathcal{A}\rightarrow\{0,1\}</annotation></semantics></math> is the binary reward function. There is no state transition function, which is denoted as <math id="S2.SS3.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\emptyset" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">∅</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\emptyset</annotation></semantics></math>. The horizon <math id="S2.SS3.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathit{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn class="ltx_mathvariant_italic" mathvariant="italic">1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathit{1}</annotation></semantics></math> is one-step in the single-turn setting. Initial state is the same as in <math id="S2.SS3.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.Thmdefinition4" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_definition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S2.Thmdefinition4.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Definition 2.4</span></span><span id="S2.Thmdefinition4.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Reward function from expert trajectory)<span id="S2.Thmdefinition4.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S2.Thmdefinition4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.Thmdefinition4.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.Thmdefinition4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">The binary reward function <math id="S2.Thmdefinition4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r_{\pi^{GT}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>r</mi><msup><mi>π</mi><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow></msup></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r_{\pi^{GT}}</annotation></semantics></math> for single-turn MDP <math id="S2.Thmdefinition4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}_{S}=(\mathcal{S},\mathcal{A},\emptyset,r_{\pi^{GT}},\mathit{1},s^{GT})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mi>S</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mo>,</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∅</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><msup><mi>π</mi><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow></msup></msub><mo>,</mo><mn class="ltx_mathvariant_italic" mathvariant="italic">1</mn><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>s</mi><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}_{S}=(\mathcal{S},\mathcal{A},\emptyset,r_{\pi^{GT}},\mathit{1},s^{GT})</annotation></semantics></math> is defined as:</span></p>
<table id="S2.EGx3" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S2.E3"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.E3.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\displaystyle r_{\pi^{GT}}(s,a)=\mathds{1}\{a=\pi^{GT}(s)\}\in\{0,1\}," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><msup><mi>π</mi><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow></msup></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>𝟙</mn><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>π</mi><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow></msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle r_{\pi^{GT}}(s,a)=\mathds{1}\{a=\pi^{GT}(s)\}\in\{0,1\},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(3)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.Thmdefinition4.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.Thmdefinition4.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">where <math id="S2.Thmdefinition4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi^{GT}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>π</mi><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi^{GT}</annotation></semantics></math> is the expert policy defined in <a href="#S2.Thmdefinition3" title="Definition 2.3 (Expert policy from expert trajectory). ‣ 2.2 Expert Trajectory with Unique Optimality ‣ 2 Problem Formulation ‣ Training Task Reasoning LLM Agents for Multi-turn Task Planning via Single-turn Reinforcement Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Definition</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.3</span></a>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">The single-turn MDP <math id="S2.SS3.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}_{S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mi>S</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}_{S}</annotation></semantics></math> is based on expert trajectories <math id="S2.SS3.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau^{GT}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>τ</mi><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau^{GT}</annotation></semantics></math>, which enables efficient policy optimization with dense reward in <a href="#S2.Thmdefinition4" title="Definition 2.4 (Reward function from expert trajectory). ‣ 2.3 Single-Turn Task Reasoning MDP ‣ 2 Problem Formulation ‣ Training Task Reasoning LLM Agents for Multi-turn Task Planning via Single-turn Reinforcement Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Definition</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.4</span></a>. From the methodology section below, we will show that good performance on single-turn task reasoning (high reward <math id="S2.SS3.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>r</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r</annotation></semantics></math> in <math id="S2.SS3.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}_{S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mi>S</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}_{S}</annotation></semantics></math>) will lead to successful long-horizon task planning (high reward <math id="S2.SS3.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>R</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R</annotation></semantics></math> in <math id="S2.SS3.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}</annotation></semantics></math>).</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S3" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">3 </span>Methodology</h2>

<div id="S3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We first give the theoretical analysis of policy learning for single-turn MDP <math id="S3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}_{S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mi>S</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}_{S}</annotation></semantics></math> based on expert trajectories. Then we define the success probability with the minimal turns for task planning MDP <math id="S3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}</annotation></semantics></math> and show that policy improvement from <math id="S3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}_{S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mi>S</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}_{S}</annotation></semantics></math> can lead to higher success probability in <math id="S3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}</annotation></semantics></math>. Finally, we show that the learned policy can generalize well to unseen task planning with fewer completion turns.</p>
</div>
<section id="S3.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.1 </span>GRPO for Single-Turn Task Reasoning</h3>

<div id="S3.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">GRPO is an on-policy model-free RL method that optimizes policies using verifiable rewards and replaces critic model with a group of multiple responses for a prompt to calculate a group-based advantage <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">17</a>, <a href="#bib.bib18" title="" class="ltx_ref">18</a>]</cite>.
Based on the theoretical foundation of GRPO <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib19" title="" class="ltx_ref">19</a>]</cite>,
we give the policy iteration and the success amplification
in the single-turn MDP <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}_{S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mi>S</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}_{S}</annotation></semantics></math> defined with <a href="#S2.Thmdefinition4" title="Definition 2.4 (Reward function from expert trajectory). ‣ 2.3 Single-Turn Task Reasoning MDP ‣ 2 Problem Formulation ‣ Training Task Reasoning LLM Agents for Multi-turn Task Planning via Single-turn Reinforcement Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Definition</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.4</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<section id="S3.SS1.SSS1" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">3.1.1 </span>GRPO Policy Iteration Dynamics</h4>

<div id="S3.SS1.SSS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.SSS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="S3.SS1.SSS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho_{Q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ρ</mi><mi>Q</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho_{Q}</annotation></semantics></math> be a distribution over state query prompts <math id="S3.SS1.SSS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s\in\mathcal{S}\sim\rho_{Q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mo>∼</mo><msub><mi>ρ</mi><mi>Q</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s\in\mathcal{S}\sim\rho_{Q}</annotation></semantics></math>. For the single-turn MDP <math id="S3.SS1.SSS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}_{S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mi>S</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}_{S}</annotation></semantics></math>, we consider the GRPO optimization problem which adopts the relative advantage within a group of policy outputs as the critic while maintaining proximity to a reference policy through KL regularization.
The GRPO objective without clipping is formulated as:</p>
<table id="S3.EGx4" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S3.E4"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E4.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\displaystyle\max_{\pi}\mathbb{E}_{s\sim\rho_{Q}}\mathbb{E}_{a\sim\pi_{\text{old}}(\cdot|s)}\frac{\pi(a|s)}{\pi_{\text{old}}(a|s)}A(s,a)-\beta\text{KL}(\pi||\pi^{\text{ref}})," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><munder><mi>max</mi><mi>π</mi></munder><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>∼</mo><msub><mi>ρ</mi><mi>Q</mi></msub></mrow></msub><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>∼</mo><mi>π</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mtext>old</mtext></msub><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msub><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><mi>π</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mtext>old</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mi>A</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>β</mi><mtext>KL</mtext><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>π</mi><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><msup><mi>π</mi><mtext>ref</mtext></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\max_{\pi}\mathbb{E}_{s\sim\rho_{Q}}\mathbb{E}_{a\sim\pi_{\text{old}}(\cdot|s)}\frac{\pi(a|s)}{\pi_{\text{old}}(a|s)}A(s,a)-\beta\text{KL}(\pi||\pi^{\text{ref}}),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(4)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS1.SSS1.p1.2" class="ltx_p">where the advantage function <math id="S3.SS1.SSS1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math> based on the binary reward in <a href="#S2.Thmdefinition4" title="Definition 2.4 (Reward function from expert trajectory). ‣ 2.3 Single-Turn Task Reasoning MDP ‣ 2 Problem Formulation ‣ Training Task Reasoning LLM Agents for Multi-turn Task Planning via Single-turn Reinforcement Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Definition</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.4</span></a> is given by:</p>
<table id="S3.EGx5" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S3.E5"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle A_{\pi^{GT}}(s,a)=\frac{r_{\pi^{GT}}(s,a)-\mathbb{E}_{a^{\prime}\sim\pi_{\text{old}}(\cdot|s)}r_{\pi^{GT}}(s,a^{\prime})}{\sqrt{\text{Var}_{a^{\prime}\sim\pi_{\text{old}}(\cdot|s)}r_{\pi^{GT}}(s,a^{\prime})}}." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><msup><mi>π</mi><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow></msup></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><mi>r</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><msup><mi>π</mi><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow></msup></msub><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo>−</mo><mi>𝔼</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>a</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>∼</mo><mi>π</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mtext>old</mtext></msub><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msub><mi>r</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><msup><mi>π</mi><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow></msup></msub><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>a</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><msqrt><mrow><mtext>Var</mtext><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>a</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>∼</mo><mi>π</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mtext>old</mtext></msub><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msub><mi>r</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><msup><mi>π</mi><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow></msup></msub><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>a</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msqrt></mfrac></mstyle></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle A_{\pi^{GT}}(s,a)=\frac{r_{\pi^{GT}}(s,a)-\mathbb{E}_{a^{\prime}\sim\pi_{\text{old}}(\cdot|s)}r_{\pi^{GT}}(s,a^{\prime})}{\sqrt{\text{Var}_{a^{\prime}\sim\pi_{\text{old}}(\cdot|s)}r_{\pi^{GT}}(s,a^{\prime})}}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(5)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS1.SSS1.p1.3" class="ltx_p">At iteration <math id="S3.SS1.SSS1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n\geq 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n\geq 1</annotation></semantics></math>, the policy iteration of GRPO for <math id="S3.SS1.SSS1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}_{S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mi>S</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}_{S}</annotation></semantics></math> is,</p>
<table id="S3.EGx6" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S3.E6"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E6.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\displaystyle\pi_{n}=\arg\max_{\pi}\mathbb{E}_{s\sim\rho_{Q}}\bigg\{\mathbb{E}_{a\sim\pi_{n-1}(\cdot|s)}\bigg[\frac{\pi_{n}(a|s)}{\pi_{n-1}(a|s)}\frac{r_{\pi^{GT}}(s,a)-\mathbb{E}_{a^{\prime}\sim\pi_{n-1}(\cdot|s)}r_{\pi^{GT}}(s,a^{\prime})}{\sqrt{\text{Var}_{a^{\prime}\sim\pi_{n-1}(\cdot|s)}r_{\pi^{GT}}(s,a^{\prime})}}\bigg]-\beta\text{KL}(\pi_{n}||\pi^{\text{ref}})\bigg\}," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>arg</mi><munder><mi>max</mi><mi>π</mi></munder><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>∼</mo><msub><mi>ρ</mi><mi>Q</mi></msub></mrow></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>∼</mo><mi>π</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msub><mrow><mo maxsize="2.100em" minsize="2.100em">[</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><mi>r</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><msup><mi>π</mi><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow></msup></msub><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo>−</mo><mi>𝔼</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>a</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>∼</mo><mi>π</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msub><mi>r</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><msup><mi>π</mi><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow></msup></msub><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>a</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><msqrt><mrow><mtext>Var</mtext><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>a</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>∼</mo><mi>π</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msub><mi>r</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><msup><mi>π</mi><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow></msup></msub><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>a</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msqrt></mfrac></mstyle><mo maxsize="2.100em" minsize="2.100em">]</mo></mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>β</mi><mtext>KL</mtext><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><msup><mi>π</mi><mtext>ref</mtext></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\pi_{n}=\arg\max_{\pi}\mathbb{E}_{s\sim\rho_{Q}}\bigg\{\mathbb{E}_{a\sim\pi_{n-1}(\cdot|s)}\bigg[\frac{\pi_{n}(a|s)}{\pi_{n-1}(a|s)}\frac{r_{\pi^{GT}}(s,a)-\mathbb{E}_{a^{\prime}\sim\pi_{n-1}(\cdot|s)}r_{\pi^{GT}}(s,a^{\prime})}{\sqrt{\text{Var}_{a^{\prime}\sim\pi_{n-1}(\cdot|s)}r_{\pi^{GT}}(s,a^{\prime})}}\bigg]-\beta\text{KL}(\pi_{n}||\pi^{\text{ref}})\bigg\},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(6)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS1.SSS1.p1.4" class="ltx_p">where initial policy is the reference policy <math id="S3.SS1.SSS1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{0}=\pi^{\text{ref}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>π</mi><mtext>ref</mtext></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{0}=\pi^{\text{ref}}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS1.SSS2" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">3.1.2 </span>GRPO Single-Turn Optimality and Success Amplification</h4>

<div id="S3.SS1.SSS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.SSS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Following the <a href="#S3.Thmtheorem1" title="Theorem 3.1 (Theorem 3 from []). ‣ 3.1.2 GRPO Single-Turn Optimality and Success Amplification ‣ 3.1 GRPO for Single-Turn Task Reasoning ‣ 3 Methodology ‣ Training Task Reasoning LLM Agents for Multi-turn Task Planning via Single-turn Reinforcement Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Theorem</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.1</span></a> from <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib19" title="" class="ltx_ref">19</a>]</cite> below, we can have the optimality guarantee for GRPO iteration in <a href="#S3.E6" title="In 3.1.1 GRPO Policy Iteration Dynamics ‣ 3.1 GRPO for Single-Turn Task Reasoning ‣ 3 Methodology ‣ Training Task Reasoning LLM Agents for Multi-turn Task Planning via Single-turn Reinforcement Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Equation</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a> in our single-turn task reasoning setting.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_theorem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 3.1</span></span><span id="S3.Thmtheorem1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Theorem 3 from <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib19" title="" class="ltx_ref">19</a>]</cite>)<span id="S3.Thmtheorem1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.Thmtheorem1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="S3.Thmtheorem1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>π</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi^{*}</annotation></semantics></math> be the fixed point of GRPO-optimized policy obtained from the policy iteration dynamics of <a href="#S3.E6" title="In 3.1.1 GRPO Policy Iteration Dynamics ‣ 3.1 GRPO for Single-Turn Task Reasoning ‣ 3 Methodology ‣ Training Task Reasoning LLM Agents for Multi-turn Task Planning via Single-turn Reinforcement Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Equation</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a> and <math id="S3.Thmtheorem1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi^{\text{ref}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>π</mi><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">ref</mtext></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi^{\text{ref}}</annotation></semantics></math> be the initial reference policy. Denote the success probability of the reference policy as <math id="S3.Thmtheorem1.p1.m3" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="p^{\text{ref}}=\mathbb{E}_{a\sim\pi^{\text{ref}}(\cdot|s^{GT})}[\mathds{1}\{a=\pi^{GT}(s^{GT})\}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>p</mi><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">ref</mtext></msup><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>∼</mo><mi>π</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">ref</mtext></msup><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>s</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mn>𝟙</mn><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>π</mi><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow></msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>s</mi><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p^{\text{ref}}=\mathbb{E}_{a\sim\pi^{\text{ref}}(\cdot|s^{GT})}[\mathds{1}\{a=\pi^{GT}(s^{GT})\}]</annotation></semantics></math> and optimized policy as <math id="S3.Thmtheorem1.p1.m4" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="p^{*}=\mathbb{E}_{a\sim\pi^{*}(\cdot|s^{GT})}[\mathds{1}\{a=\pi^{GT}(s^{GT})\}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>p</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>∼</mo><mi>π</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>s</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mn>𝟙</mn><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>π</mi><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow></msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>s</mi><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p^{*}=\mathbb{E}_{a\sim\pi^{*}(\cdot|s^{GT})}[\mathds{1}\{a=\pi^{GT}(s^{GT})\}]</annotation></semantics></math>. It holds that <math id="S3.Thmtheorem1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p^{*}&gt;p^{\text{ref}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>p</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo>&gt;</mo><msup><mi>p</mi><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">ref</mtext></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p^{*}&gt;p^{\text{ref}}</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.SSS2.2" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S3.SS1.SSS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.SSS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS1.SSS2.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text">Proof can be found in Appendix D of <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib19" title="" class="ltx_ref">19</a>]</cite>.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.Thmcorollary1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_corollary">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S3.Thmcorollary1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Corollary 3.1</span></span><span id="S3.Thmcorollary1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(GRPO single-Turn optimality)<span id="S3.Thmcorollary1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S3.Thmcorollary1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.Thmcorollary1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.Thmcorollary1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">For the fixed point of GRPO-optimized policy <math id="S3.Thmcorollary1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>π</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi^{*}</annotation></semantics></math> and the reference policy <math id="S3.Thmcorollary1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi^{\text{ref}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>π</mi><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">ref</mtext></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi^{\text{ref}}</annotation></semantics></math>, the following holds for any state <math id="S3.Thmcorollary1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>s</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s</annotation></semantics></math> from the expert trajectory <math id="S3.Thmcorollary1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau^{GT}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>τ</mi><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau^{GT}</annotation></semantics></math>,</span></p>
<table id="S3.EGx7" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S3.E7"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E7.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\displaystyle\mathbb{E}_{a^{*}\sim\pi^{*}(\cdot|s)}[r_{\pi^{GT}}(s,a^{*})]\geq\mathbb{E}_{a^{\text{ref}}\sim\pi^{\text{ref}}(\cdot|s)}[r_{\pi^{GT}}(s,a^{\text{ref}})]." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><mi>a</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo>∼</mo><mi>π</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><msup><mi>π</mi><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow></msup></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>a</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>≥</mo><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><mi>a</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">ref</mtext></msup><mo>∼</mo><mi>π</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">ref</mtext></msup><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><msup><mi>π</mi><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow></msup></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>a</mi><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">ref</mtext></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathbb{E}_{a^{*}\sim\pi^{*}(\cdot|s)}[r_{\pi^{GT}}(s,a^{*})]\geq\mathbb{E}_{a^{\text{ref}}\sim\pi^{\text{ref}}(\cdot|s)}[r_{\pi^{GT}}(s,a^{\text{ref}})].</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(7)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.SSS2.3" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S3.SS1.SSS2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.SSS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS1.SSS2.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text">It holds based on the single-turn reward formulation in <a href="#S2.Thmdefinition4" title="Definition 2.4 (Reward function from expert trajectory). ‣ 2.3 Single-Turn Task Reasoning MDP ‣ 2 Problem Formulation ‣ Training Task Reasoning LLM Agents for Multi-turn Task Planning via Single-turn Reinforcement Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Definition</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.4</span></a> based on <a href="#S3.Thmtheorem1" title="Theorem 3.1 (Theorem 3 from []). ‣ 3.1.2 GRPO Single-Turn Optimality and Success Amplification ‣ 3.1 GRPO for Single-Turn Task Reasoning ‣ 3 Methodology ‣ Training Task Reasoning LLM Agents for Multi-turn Task Planning via Single-turn Reinforcement Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Theorem</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.1</span></a>.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.SSS2.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.SSS2.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><a href="#S3.Thmcorollary1" title="Corollary 3.1 (GRPO single-Turn optimality). ‣ 3.1.2 GRPO Single-Turn Optimality and Success Amplification ‣ 3.1 GRPO for Single-Turn Task Reasoning ‣ 3 Methodology ‣ Training Task Reasoning LLM Agents for Multi-turn Task Planning via Single-turn Reinforcement Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Corollary</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.1</span></a> establishes that GRPO training on the single-turn MDP <math id="S3.SS1.SSS2.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}_{S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mi>S</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}_{S}</annotation></semantics></math> with expert trajectories leads to policies that achieve higher success rates than the reference policy before GRPO training, providing the foundation for analyzing multi-turn performance improvements.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.2 </span>Success Probability for Multi-Turn Task Planning</h3>

<div id="S3.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">To analyze how GRPO improvements on the single-turn MDP <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}_{S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mi>S</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}_{S}</annotation></semantics></math> can result in performance gains in the multi-turn MDP <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}</annotation></semantics></math>, we introduce the success probability with the minimal turns that captures the probability of achieving the optimal expert trajectory, by assuming the existence of minimal completion steps given any state below.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.Thmdefinition1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_definition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S3.Thmdefinition1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Definition 3.1</span></span><span id="S3.Thmdefinition1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Minimal turns for task completion)<span id="S3.Thmdefinition1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S3.Thmdefinition1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.Thmdefinition1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.Thmdefinition1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">For any state <math id="S3.Thmdefinition1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s\in\mathcal{S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s\in\mathcal{S}</annotation></semantics></math> in the multi-turn MDP <math id="S3.Thmdefinition1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}</annotation></semantics></math>, denote the unique minimal length of turns as <math id="S3.Thmdefinition1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T^{*}(s)\geq 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>T</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T^{*}(s)\geq 0</annotation></semantics></math> among all successful trajectories defined in <a href="#S2.Thmdefinition1" title="Definition 2.1 (Successful trajectory by stochastic policy). ‣ 2.1 Multi-Turn Task Planning MDP ‣ 2 Problem Formulation ‣ Training Task Reasoning LLM Agents for Multi-turn Task Planning via Single-turn Reinforcement Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Definition</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.1</span></a> from <math id="S3.Thmdefinition1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>s</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s</annotation></semantics></math>, i.e., <math id="S3.Thmdefinition1.p1.m5" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="T^{*}(s):=\min\{T\geq 0:\exists\tau(T)\text{ s.t. }R(s_{T},a_{T})=1,s_{0}=s\}." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>T</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>:=</mo><mi>min</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi>T</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mo rspace="0.167em">∃</mo><mi>τ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic"> s.t. </mtext><mi>R</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T^{*}(s):=\min\{T\geq 0:\exists\tau(T)\text{ s.t. }R(s_{T},a_{T})=1,s_{0}=s\}.</annotation></semantics></math></span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S3.SS2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T^{*}(s)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>T</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T^{*}(s)</annotation></semantics></math> exists uniquely based on the existence and uniqueness of the expert trajectory in <a href="#S2.Thmdefinition2" title="Definition 2.2 (Expert trajectory by stochastic policy). ‣ 2.2 Expert Trajectory with Unique Optimality ‣ 2 Problem Formulation ‣ Training Task Reasoning LLM Agents for Multi-turn Task Planning via Single-turn Reinforcement Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Definition</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.2</span></a> by taking any states as initial states. We then define the probability of the successful trajectory with minimal steps.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.Thmdefinition2" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_definition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S3.Thmdefinition2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Definition 3.2</span></span><span id="S3.Thmdefinition2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Success probability with minimal steps)<span id="S3.Thmdefinition2.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S3.Thmdefinition2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.Thmdefinition2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.Thmdefinition2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">With <math id="S3.Thmdefinition2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T^{*}(s)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>T</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T^{*}(s)</annotation></semantics></math> in <a href="#S3.Thmdefinition1" title="Definition 3.1 (Minimal turns for task completion). ‣ 3.2 Success Probability for Multi-Turn Task Planning ‣ 3 Methodology ‣ Training Task Reasoning LLM Agents for Multi-turn Task Planning via Single-turn Reinforcement Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Definition</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.1</span></a>, the probability of achieving the task planning goal in the minimal number of steps starting from state <math id="S3.Thmdefinition2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> at time <math id="S3.Thmdefinition2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math> under policy <math id="S3.Thmdefinition2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>π</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi</annotation></semantics></math> in the multi-turn MDP <math id="S3.Thmdefinition2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}</annotation></semantics></math> is defined as,</span></p>
<table id="S3.EGx8" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S3.E8"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle P_{t}^{\pi}(s_{t})=\mathbb{P}_{\pi}\left(R(s_{t+T^{*}(s_{t})},a_{t+T^{*}(s_{t})})=1|s_{t}\right)." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>P</mi><mi>t</mi><mi>π</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>ℙ</mi><mi>π</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mrow><msup><mi>T</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mrow><msup><mi>T</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle P_{t}^{\pi}(s_{t})=\mathbb{P}_{\pi}\left(R(s_{t+T^{*}(s_{t})},a_{t+T^{*}(s_{t})})=1|s_{t}\right).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(8)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">We then present the recursion condition for the success probability in <a href="#S3.Thmdefinition2" title="Definition 3.2 (Success probability with minimal steps). ‣ 3.2 Success Probability for Multi-Turn Task Planning ‣ 3 Methodology ‣ Training Task Reasoning LLM Agents for Multi-turn Task Planning via Single-turn Reinforcement Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Definition</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.2</span></a> based on the reward function from the expert trajectory in <a href="#S2.Thmdefinition4" title="Definition 2.4 (Reward function from expert trajectory). ‣ 2.3 Single-Turn Task Reasoning MDP ‣ 2 Problem Formulation ‣ Training Task Reasoning LLM Agents for Multi-turn Task Planning via Single-turn Reinforcement Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Definition</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.4</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem2" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_theorem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 3.2</span></span><span id="S3.Thmtheorem2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Recursion equation of success probability)<span id="S3.Thmtheorem2.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.Thmtheorem2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">The success probability defined in <a href="#S3.Thmdefinition2" title="Definition 3.2 (Success probability with minimal steps). ‣ 3.2 Success Probability for Multi-Turn Task Planning ‣ 3 Methodology ‣ Training Task Reasoning LLM Agents for Multi-turn Task Planning via Single-turn Reinforcement Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Definition</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.2</span></a> satisfies the following recursion condition,</span></p>
<table id="S3.EGx9" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S3.E9"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E9.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\displaystyle P_{t}^{\pi}(s_{t})=\mathbb{E}_{a\sim\pi(\cdot|s_{t})}[r_{\pi^{GT}}(s_{t},a)\cdot P_{t+1}^{\pi}(s_{t+1})]," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>P</mi><mi>t</mi><mi>π</mi></msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>∼</mo><mi>π</mi><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>s</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><msup><mi>π</mi><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow></msup></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>a</mi><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><msubsup><mi>P</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>π</mi></msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle P_{t}^{\pi}(s_{t})=\mathbb{E}_{a\sim\pi(\cdot|s_{t})}[r_{\pi^{GT}}(s_{t},a)\cdot P_{t+1}^{\pi}(s_{t+1})],</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(9)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.Thmtheorem2.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem2.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">where <math id="S3.Thmtheorem2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi^{GT}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>π</mi><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi^{GT}</annotation></semantics></math> is the expert policy in <a href="#S2.Thmdefinition3" title="Definition 2.3 (Expert policy from expert trajectory). ‣ 2.2 Expert Trajectory with Unique Optimality ‣ 2 Problem Formulation ‣ Training Task Reasoning LLM Agents for Multi-turn Task Planning via Single-turn Reinforcement Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Definition</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.3</span></a> by expert trajectory with <math id="S3.Thmtheorem2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{t+T^{*}(s_{t})}^{\pi}(s_{t+T^{*}(s_{t})})=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>P</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mrow><msup><mi>T</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mi>π</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mrow><msup><mi>T</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{t+T^{*}(s_{t})}^{\pi}(s_{t+T^{*}(s_{t})})=1</annotation></semantics></math> for any state <math id="S3.Thmtheorem2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s_{t}</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.2" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S3.SS2.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS2.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text">For any state <math id="S3.SS2.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> as the initial state, based on <a href="#S2.Thmdefinition3" title="Definition 2.3 (Expert policy from expert trajectory). ‣ 2.2 Expert Trajectory with Unique Optimality ‣ 2 Problem Formulation ‣ Training Task Reasoning LLM Agents for Multi-turn Task Planning via Single-turn Reinforcement Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Definition</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.3</span></a>, <math id="S3.SS2.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi^{GT}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>π</mi><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi^{GT}</annotation></semantics></math> is induced by the expert trajectory <math id="S3.SS2.p4.m3" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\tau^{GT}=[(s_{t},a_{t}),\dots,(s_{T^{GT}},a_{T^{GT}})],s_{k+1}=f(s_{k},\pi^{GT}(s_{k})),k=t,t+1,\dots,T^{GT}-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>τ</mi><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><msup><mi>T</mi><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow></msup></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><msup><mi>T</mi><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow></msup></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>f</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>π</mi><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow></msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>t</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>T</mi><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow></msup><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau^{GT}=[(s_{t},a_{t}),\dots,(s_{T^{GT}},a_{T^{GT}})],s_{k+1}=f(s_{k},\pi^{GT}(s_{k})),k=t,t+1,\dots,T^{GT}-1</annotation></semantics></math> with minimal turns of task completion. By <math id="S3.SS2.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{t+T^{*}(s_{t})}^{\pi}(s_{t+T^{*}(s_{t})})=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>P</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mrow><msup><mi>T</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mi>π</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mrow><msup><mi>T</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{t+T^{*}(s_{t})}^{\pi}(s_{t+T^{*}(s_{t})})=1</annotation></semantics></math>, we have <math id="S3.SS2.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R(s_{t+T^{*}(s_{t})},a_{t+T^{*}(s_{t})})=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mrow><msup><mi>T</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mrow><msup><mi>T</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R(s_{t+T^{*}(s_{t})},a_{t+T^{*}(s_{t})})=1</annotation></semantics></math>. Combining the uniqueness of expert trajectory in <a href="#S2.Thmdefinition2" title="Definition 2.2 (Expert trajectory by stochastic policy). ‣ 2.2 Expert Trajectory with Unique Optimality ‣ 2 Problem Formulation ‣ Training Task Reasoning LLM Agents for Multi-turn Task Planning via Single-turn Reinforcement Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Definition</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.2</span></a> and minimal completion steps <math id="S3.SS2.p4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T^{*}(s)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>T</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T^{*}(s)</annotation></semantics></math> in <a href="#S3.Thmdefinition1" title="Definition 3.1 (Minimal turns for task completion). ‣ 3.2 Success Probability for Multi-Turn Task Planning ‣ 3 Methodology ‣ Training Task Reasoning LLM Agents for Multi-turn Task Planning via Single-turn Reinforcement Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Definition</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.1</span></a>, it holds that</span></p>
<table id="S3.EGx10" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S3.E10"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E10.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\displaystyle T^{*}(s_{T^{GT}})=0,T^{GT}=T^{*}(s_{k})+k,k=t,\dots,T^{GT}." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>T</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><msup><mi>T</mi><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow></msup></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>T</mi><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow></msup><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>T</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>+</mo><mi>k</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>t</mi><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>T</mi><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle T^{*}(s_{T^{GT}})=0,T^{GT}=T^{*}(s_{k})+k,k=t,\dots,T^{GT}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(10)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS2.p4.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS2.p4.2.1" class="ltx_text">For any state that deviates from the expert trajectory <math id="S3.SS2.p4.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s_{k+1}^{\prime}\neq f(s_{k},\pi^{GT}(s_{k}))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>s</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo>≠</mo><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><msup><mi>π</mi><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s_{k+1}^{\prime}\neq f(s_{k},\pi^{GT}(s_{k}))</annotation></semantics></math>, by the minimal completion turns,</span></p>
<table id="S3.EGx11" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S3.E11"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E11.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\displaystyle T^{*}(s_{k+1}^{\prime})&gt;T^{*}(s_{k})-1,\forall k=t,t+1,\dots,T^{GT}-1." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>T</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>s</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><msup><mi>T</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mo rspace="0.167em">∀</mo><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>t</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>T</mi><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow></msup><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle T^{*}(s_{k+1}^{\prime})&gt;T^{*}(s_{k})-1,\forall k=t,t+1,\dots,T^{GT}-1.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(11)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS2.p4.3" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS2.p4.3.1" class="ltx_text">By the law of total expectation over the next action <math id="S3.SS2.p4.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t+1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t+1</annotation></semantics></math>:</span></p>
<table id="S3.EGx12" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S3.E12"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E12.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle P_{t}^{\pi}(s_{t})=\mathbb{P}_{\pi}(R(s_{t+T^{*}(s_{t})},a_{t+T^{*}(s_{t})})=1|s_{t})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>P</mi><mi>t</mi><mi>π</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>ℙ</mi><mi>π</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mrow><msup><mi>T</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mrow><msup><mi>T</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle P_{t}^{\pi}(s_{t})=\mathbb{P}_{\pi}(R(s_{t+T^{*}(s_{t})},a_{t+T^{*}(s_{t})})=1|s_{t})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(12)</span></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S3.Ex1"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.Ex1.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\displaystyle=\mathbb{E}_{a\sim\pi(\cdot|s_{t})}[\mathbb{P}_{\pi}(R(s_{t+T^{*}(s_{t})},a_{t+T^{*}(s_{t})})=1|s_{t},a)]." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>∼</mo><mi>π</mi><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>s</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msub><mi>ℙ</mi><mi>π</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>R</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mrow><msup><mi>T</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mrow><msup><mi>T</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\mathbb{E}_{a\sim\pi(\cdot|s_{t})}[\mathbb{P}_{\pi}(R(s_{t+T^{*}(s_{t})},a_{t+T^{*}(s_{t})})=1|s_{t},a)].</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS2.p4.4" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS2.p4.4.1" class="ltx_text">For <math id="S3.SS2.p4.m9" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="a\sim\pi(\cdot|s_{t})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>∼</mo><mi>π</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a\sim\pi(\cdot|s_{t})</annotation></semantics></math>, if <math id="S3.SS2.p4.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a\neq\pi^{GT}(s_{t}),i.e.,r_{\pi^{GT}}(s_{t},a)=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>≠</mo><mrow><msup><mi>π</mi><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">.</mo><mi>e</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">.</mo><mo>,</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><msup><mi>π</mi><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow></msup></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a\neq\pi^{GT}(s_{t}),i.e.,r_{\pi^{GT}}(s_{t},a)=0</annotation></semantics></math>, we have <math id="S3.SS2.p4.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s_{t+1}^{\prime}\neq f(s_{t},\pi(s_{t}))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>s</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo>≠</mo><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>π</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s_{t+1}^{\prime}\neq f(s_{t},\pi(s_{t}))</annotation></semantics></math>. Then based on <a href="#S3.E11" title="In Proof. ‣ 3.2 Success Probability for Multi-Turn Task Planning ‣ 3 Methodology ‣ Training Task Reasoning LLM Agents for Multi-turn Task Planning via Single-turn Reinforcement Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Equation</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">11</span></a>, it takes strictly more than <math id="S3.SS2.p4.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T^{*}(s_{t})-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>T</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T^{*}(s_{t})-1</annotation></semantics></math> steps for completion, i.e.,</span></p>
<table id="S3.EGx13" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S3.E13"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E13.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\mathbb{P}_{\pi}(R(s_{t+1+T^{*}(s_{t})-1},a_{t+1+T^{*}(s_{t})-1})=1|s_{t+1}^{\prime})=0." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>ℙ</mi><mi>π</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mrow><msup><mi>T</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mrow><msup><mi>T</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><msubsup><mi>s</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathbb{P}_{\pi}(R(s_{t+1+T^{*}(s_{t})-1},a_{t+1+T^{*}(s_{t})-1})=1|s_{t+1}^{\prime})=0.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(13)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS2.p4.5" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS2.p4.5.1" class="ltx_text">If <math id="S3.SS2.p4.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a=\pi^{GT}(s_{t}),i.e.,r_{\pi^{GT}}(s_{t},a)=1,s_{t+1}=f(s_{t},a)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mi>π</mi><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">.</mo><mi>e</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">.</mo><mo>,</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><msup><mi>π</mi><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow></msup></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a=\pi^{GT}(s_{t}),i.e.,r_{\pi^{GT}}(s_{t},a)=1,s_{t+1}=f(s_{t},a)</annotation></semantics></math>, then based on <a href="#S3.E10" title="In Proof. ‣ 3.2 Success Probability for Multi-Turn Task Planning ‣ 3 Methodology ‣ Training Task Reasoning LLM Agents for Multi-turn Task Planning via Single-turn Reinforcement Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Equation</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">10</span></a>, we have</span></p>
<table id="S3.EGx14" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S3.E14"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E14.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\mathbb{P}_{\pi}(R(s_{t+T^{*}(s_{t})},a_{t+T^{*}(s_{t})})=1|s_{t},a)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ℙ</mi><mi>π</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mrow><msup><mi>T</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mrow><msup><mi>T</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>a</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathbb{P}_{\pi}(R(s_{t+T^{*}(s_{t})},a_{t+T^{*}(s_{t})})=1|s_{t},a)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(14)</span></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S3.Ex2"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.Ex2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>=</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.Ex2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\mathbb{P}_{\pi}(R(s_{t+1+T^{*}(s_{t+1})},a_{t+1+T^{*}(s_{t+1})})=1|s_{t+1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ℙ</mi><mi>π</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mrow><msup><mi>T</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mrow><msup><mi>T</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathbb{P}_{\pi}(R(s_{t+1+T^{*}(s_{t+1})},a_{t+1+T^{*}(s_{t+1})})=1|s_{t+1})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S3.Ex3"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.Ex3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>=</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.Ex3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle P_{t+1}^{\pi}(s_{t+1})." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>P</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>π</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle P_{t+1}^{\pi}(s_{t+1}).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS2.p4.6" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS2.p4.6.1" class="ltx_text">With the values of <math id="S3.SS2.p4.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r_{\pi^{GT}}(s_{t},a)\in\{0,1\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><msup><mi>π</mi><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow></msup></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r_{\pi^{GT}}(s_{t},a)\in\{0,1\}</annotation></semantics></math> and combining <a href="#S3.E12" title="In Proof. ‣ 3.2 Success Probability for Multi-Turn Task Planning ‣ 3 Methodology ‣ Training Task Reasoning LLM Agents for Multi-turn Task Planning via Single-turn Reinforcement Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Equations</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">12</span></a>, <a href="#S3.E13" title="Equation 13 ‣ Proof. ‣ 3.2 Success Probability for Multi-Turn Task Planning ‣ 3 Methodology ‣ Training Task Reasoning LLM Agents for Multi-turn Task Planning via Single-turn Reinforcement Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">13</span></a> and <a href="#S3.E14" title="Equation 14 ‣ Proof. ‣ 3.2 Success Probability for Multi-Turn Task Planning ‣ 3 Methodology ‣ Training Task Reasoning LLM Agents for Multi-turn Task Planning via Single-turn Reinforcement Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">14</span></a>, it holds that</span></p>
<table id="S3.EGx15" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S3.E15"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E15.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\displaystyle P_{t}^{\pi}(s_{t})=\mathbb{E}_{a\sim\pi(\cdot|s_{t})}[r_{\pi^{GT}}(s_{t},a)\cdot P_{t+1}^{\pi}(s_{t+1})]," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>P</mi><mi>t</mi><mi>π</mi></msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>∼</mo><mi>π</mi><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>s</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><msup><mi>π</mi><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow></msup></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>a</mi><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><msubsup><mi>P</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>π</mi></msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle P_{t}^{\pi}(s_{t})=\mathbb{E}_{a\sim\pi(\cdot|s_{t})}[r_{\pi^{GT}}(s_{t},a)\cdot P_{t+1}^{\pi}(s_{t+1})],</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(15)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS2.p4.7" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS2.p4.7.1" class="ltx_text">which concludes the proof.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p5.1" class="ltx_p">Please note that the recursion equation in <a href="#S3.Thmtheorem2" title="Theorem 3.2 (Recursion equation of success probability). ‣ 3.2 Success Probability for Multi-Turn Task Planning ‣ 3 Methodology ‣ Training Task Reasoning LLM Agents for Multi-turn Task Planning via Single-turn Reinforcement Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Theorem</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.2</span></a> is not the same as Bellman recursion in value function iteration <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib20" title="" class="ltx_ref">20</a>]</cite>, and the key difference lies in that the multiplication of the binary single-turn reward <math id="S3.SS2.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r_{\pi^{GT}}(s_{t},a)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><msup><mi>π</mi><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow></msup></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r_{\pi^{GT}}(s_{t},a)</annotation></semantics></math> is involved in the recursion of expectation, instead of addition. This recursion bridges the final multi-turn success in <a href="#S3.E8" title="In Definition 3.2 (Success probability with minimal steps). ‣ 3.2 Success Probability for Multi-Turn Task Planning ‣ 3 Methodology ‣ Training Task Reasoning LLM Agents for Multi-turn Task Planning via Single-turn Reinforcement Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Equation</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">8</span></a> with single-turn reward improvements at each step in <a href="#S3.E7" title="In Corollary 3.1 (GRPO single-Turn optimality). ‣ 3.1.2 GRPO Single-Turn Optimality and Success Amplification ‣ 3.1 GRPO for Single-Turn Task Reasoning ‣ 3 Methodology ‣ Training Task Reasoning LLM Agents for Multi-turn Task Planning via Single-turn Reinforcement Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Equation</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7</span></a>, which is crucial to the following analysis on how single-turn GRPO improves the multi-turn task planning.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.3 </span>GRPO Improvement of Multi-Turn Success Probability</h3>

<div id="S3.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We now analyze how GRPO improvements on single-turn task reasoning in <math id="S3.SS3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}_{S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mi>S</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}_{S}</annotation></semantics></math> will lead to performance gains in the multi-turn task planning MDP <math id="S3.SS3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}</annotation></semantics></math>. First, we assume the GRPO-optimized policy for single-turn task reasoning based on expert trajectories can generalize to all states.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.Thmassumption1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_assumption">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S3.Thmassumption1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Assumption 3.1</span></span><span id="S3.Thmassumption1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(GRPO improvement on all states)<span id="S3.Thmassumption1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S3.Thmassumption1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.Thmassumption1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.Thmassumption1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">The GRPO improvement on expert trajectory states in <a href="#S3.E7" title="In Corollary 3.1 (GRPO single-Turn optimality). ‣ 3.1.2 GRPO Single-Turn Optimality and Success Amplification ‣ 3.1 GRPO for Single-Turn Task Reasoning ‣ 3 Methodology ‣ Training Task Reasoning LLM Agents for Multi-turn Task Planning via Single-turn Reinforcement Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Equation</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7</span></a> also holds for all states <math id="S3.Thmassumption1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s\in\mathcal{S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s\in\mathcal{S}</annotation></semantics></math>, i.e.,
<math id="S3.Thmassumption1.p1.m2" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\mathbb{E}_{a\sim\pi^{*}(\cdot|s)}[r_{\pi^{GT}}(s,a)]\geq\mathbb{E}_{a\sim\pi^{\text{ref}}(\cdot|s)}[r_{\pi^{GT}}(s,a)]," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>∼</mo><mi>π</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><msup><mi>π</mi><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow></msup></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>≥</mo><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>∼</mo><mi>π</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">ref</mtext></msup><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><msup><mi>π</mi><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow></msup></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{E}_{a\sim\pi^{*}(\cdot|s)}[r_{\pi^{GT}}(s,a)]\geq\mathbb{E}_{a\sim\pi^{\text{ref}}(\cdot|s)}[r_{\pi^{GT}}(s,a)],</annotation></semantics></math>
where <math id="S3.Thmassumption1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>π</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi^{*}</annotation></semantics></math> is the GRPO-optimized policy from <a href="#S3.Thmcorollary1" title="Corollary 3.1 (GRPO single-Turn optimality). ‣ 3.1.2 GRPO Single-Turn Optimality and Success Amplification ‣ 3.1 GRPO for Single-Turn Task Reasoning ‣ 3 Methodology ‣ Training Task Reasoning LLM Agents for Multi-turn Task Planning via Single-turn Reinforcement Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Corollary</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.1</span></a>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">The assumption above is a slight relaxation of <a href="#S3.Thmcorollary1" title="Corollary 3.1 (GRPO single-Turn optimality). ‣ 3.1.2 GRPO Single-Turn Optimality and Success Amplification ‣ 3.1 GRPO for Single-Turn Task Reasoning ‣ 3 Methodology ‣ Training Task Reasoning LLM Agents for Multi-turn Task Planning via Single-turn Reinforcement Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Corollary</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.1</span></a>, which empirically holds when the policy model is fine-tuned from large language models (LLMs) with strong generalizability <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib21" title="" class="ltx_ref">21</a>, <a href="#bib.bib22" title="" class="ltx_ref">22</a>]</cite>.
We then present how the GRPO will improve the multi-turn task planning with a higher success probability with the fewest turns.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem3" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_theorem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem3.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 3.3</span></span><span id="S3.Thmtheorem3.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(GRPO improves success probability)<span id="S3.Thmtheorem3.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.Thmtheorem3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="S3.Thmtheorem3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>π</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi^{*}</annotation></semantics></math> be the fixed point of the GRPO-optimized policy from the policy iteration dynamics of <a href="#S3.E6" title="In 3.1.1 GRPO Policy Iteration Dynamics ‣ 3.1 GRPO for Single-Turn Task Reasoning ‣ 3 Methodology ‣ Training Task Reasoning LLM Agents for Multi-turn Task Planning via Single-turn Reinforcement Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Equation</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a> and <math id="S3.Thmtheorem3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi^{\text{ref}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>π</mi><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">ref</mtext></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi^{\text{ref}}</annotation></semantics></math> be the initial reference policy. Under Assumption <a href="#S3.Thmassumption1" title="Assumption 3.1 (GRPO improvement on all states). ‣ 3.3 GRPO Improvement of Multi-Turn Success Probability ‣ 3 Methodology ‣ Training Task Reasoning LLM Agents for Multi-turn Task Planning via Single-turn Reinforcement Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.1</span></a> and <a href="#S3.E7" title="In Corollary 3.1 (GRPO single-Turn optimality). ‣ 3.1.2 GRPO Single-Turn Optimality and Success Amplification ‣ 3.1 GRPO for Single-Turn Task Reasoning ‣ 3 Methodology ‣ Training Task Reasoning LLM Agents for Multi-turn Task Planning via Single-turn Reinforcement Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Equation</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7</span></a>, it holds for any given state <math id="S3.Thmtheorem3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> at step <math id="S3.Thmtheorem3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math> that,</span></p>
<table id="S3.EGx16" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S3.E16"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E16.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle P_{t}^{\pi^{*}}(s_{t})\geq P_{t}^{\pi^{\text{ref}}}(s_{t})\quad\forall s_{t}\in\mathcal{S}." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>P</mi><mi>t</mi><msup><mi>π</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>P</mi><mi>t</mi><msup><mi>π</mi><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">ref</mtext></msup></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mspace style="width:1.167em;" width="1.167em"></mspace><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">∀</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle P_{t}^{\pi^{*}}(s_{t})\geq P_{t}^{\pi^{\text{ref}}}(s_{t})\quad\forall s_{t}\in\mathcal{S}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(16)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS3.2" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S3.SS3.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS3.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text">By the existence and uniqueness of the expert trajectory in <a href="#S2.Thmdefinition2" title="Definition 2.2 (Expert trajectory by stochastic policy). ‣ 2.2 Expert Trajectory with Unique Optimality ‣ 2 Problem Formulation ‣ Training Task Reasoning LLM Agents for Multi-turn Task Planning via Single-turn Reinforcement Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Definition</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.2</span></a>, denote the expert trajectory starting from <math id="S3.SS3.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> as <math id="S3.SS3.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau^{GT}=[(s_{t},a_{t}),\dots,(s_{T^{GT}},a_{T^{GT}})]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>τ</mi><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><msup><mi>T</mi><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow></msup></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><msup><mi>T</mi><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow></msup></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau^{GT}=[(s_{t},a_{t}),\dots,(s_{T^{GT}},a_{T^{GT}})]</annotation></semantics></math> with minimal turns of task completion <math id="S3.SS3.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T^{*}(s_{t})=T^{GT}-t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>T</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mi>T</mi><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow></msup><mo>−</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T^{*}(s_{t})=T^{GT}-t</annotation></semantics></math> in <a href="#S3.Thmdefinition1" title="Definition 3.1 (Minimal turns for task completion). ‣ 3.2 Success Probability for Multi-Turn Task Planning ‣ 3 Methodology ‣ Training Task Reasoning LLM Agents for Multi-turn Task Planning via Single-turn Reinforcement Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Definition</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.1</span></a>.
We prove by backward induction on the length of remaining turns to task completion. In the final case when <math id="S3.SS3.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t=T^{GT}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>T</mi><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t=T^{GT}</annotation></semantics></math>, we have <math id="S3.SS3.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{t}^{\pi^{*}}(s_{t})=P_{t}^{\pi^{\text{ref}}}(s_{t})=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>P</mi><mi>t</mi><msup><mi>π</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>P</mi><mi>t</mi><msup><mi>π</mi><mtext>ref</mtext></msup></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{t}^{\pi^{*}}(s_{t})=P_{t}^{\pi^{\text{ref}}}(s_{t})=1</annotation></semantics></math> since the task is already completed.
Now for the inductive case at step <math id="S3.SS3.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t+1,t+1\leq T^{GT}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>≤</mo><msup><mi>T</mi><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t+1,t+1\leq T^{GT}</annotation></semantics></math>, we assume <math id="S3.SS3.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{t+1}^{\pi^{*}}(s^{\prime})\geq P_{t+1}^{\pi^{\text{ref}}}(s^{\prime})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>P</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><msup><mi>π</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>s</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>P</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><msup><mi>π</mi><mtext>ref</mtext></msup></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>s</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{t+1}^{\pi^{*}}(s^{\prime})\geq P_{t+1}^{\pi^{\text{ref}}}(s^{\prime})</annotation></semantics></math> for all states <math id="S3.SS3.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>s</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math>. Then
for any state <math id="S3.SS3.p3.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> at step <math id="S3.SS3.p3.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math>, it holds that</span></p>
<table id="S3.EGx17" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S3.E17"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E17.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle P_{t}^{\pi^{*}}(s)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>P</mi><mi>t</mi><msup><mi>π</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle P_{t}^{\pi^{*}}(s)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E17.m2" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\displaystyle=\mathbb{E}_{a\sim\pi^{*}(\cdot|s)}[r_{\pi^{GT}}(s,a)\cdot P_{t+1}^{\pi^{*}}(f(s,a))]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>∼</mo><mi>π</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><msup><mi>π</mi><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow></msup></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>a</mi><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><msubsup><mi>P</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><msup><mi>π</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>f</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\mathbb{E}_{a\sim\pi^{*}(\cdot|s)}[r_{\pi^{GT}}(s,a)\cdot P_{t+1}^{\pi^{*}}(f(s,a))]</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(17)</span></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S3.E18"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E18.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\displaystyle\geq\mathbb{E}_{a\sim\pi^{*}(\cdot|s)}[r_{\pi^{GT}}(s,a)\cdot P_{t+1}^{\pi^{\text{ref}}}(f(s,a))]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>≥</mo><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>∼</mo><mi>π</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><msup><mi>π</mi><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow></msup></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>a</mi><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><msubsup><mi>P</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><msup><mi>π</mi><mtext>ref</mtext></msup></msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>f</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\geq\mathbb{E}_{a\sim\pi^{*}(\cdot|s)}[r_{\pi^{GT}}(s,a)\cdot P_{t+1}^{\pi^{\text{ref}}}(f(s,a))]</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(18)</span></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S3.E19"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E19.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\displaystyle\geq\mathbb{E}_{a\sim\pi^{\text{ref}}(\cdot|s)}[r_{\pi^{GT}}(s,a)\cdot P_{t+1}^{\pi^{\text{ref}}}(f(s,a))]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>≥</mo><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>∼</mo><mi>π</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mtext>ref</mtext></msup><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><msup><mi>π</mi><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow></msup></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>a</mi><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><msubsup><mi>P</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><msup><mi>π</mi><mtext>ref</mtext></msup></msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>f</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\geq\mathbb{E}_{a\sim\pi^{\text{ref}}(\cdot|s)}[r_{\pi^{GT}}(s,a)\cdot P_{t+1}^{\pi^{\text{ref}}}(f(s,a))]</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(19)</span></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S3.E20"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E20.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=P_{t}^{\pi^{\text{ref}}}(s)," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>P</mi><mi>t</mi><msup><mi>π</mi><mtext>ref</mtext></msup></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=P_{t}^{\pi^{\text{ref}}}(s),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(20)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS3.p3.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS3.p3.2.1" class="ltx_text">where <a href="#S3.E17" title="In Proof. ‣ 3.3 GRPO Improvement of Multi-Turn Success Probability ‣ 3 Methodology ‣ Training Task Reasoning LLM Agents for Multi-turn Task Planning via Single-turn Reinforcement Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Equations</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">17</span></a> and <a href="#S3.E20" title="Equation 20 ‣ Proof. ‣ 3.3 GRPO Improvement of Multi-Turn Success Probability ‣ 3 Methodology ‣ Training Task Reasoning LLM Agents for Multi-turn Task Planning via Single-turn Reinforcement Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">20</span></a> are from <a href="#S3.Thmtheorem2" title="Theorem 3.2 (Recursion equation of success probability). ‣ 3.2 Success Probability for Multi-Turn Task Planning ‣ 3 Methodology ‣ Training Task Reasoning LLM Agents for Multi-turn Task Planning via Single-turn Reinforcement Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Theorem</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.2</span></a>, <a href="#S3.E18" title="In Proof. ‣ 3.3 GRPO Improvement of Multi-Turn Success Probability ‣ 3 Methodology ‣ Training Task Reasoning LLM Agents for Multi-turn Task Planning via Single-turn Reinforcement Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Equation</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">18</span></a> is based on the inductive hypothesis at step <math id="S3.SS3.p3.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t+1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t+1</annotation></semantics></math> and <a href="#S3.E19" title="In Proof. ‣ 3.3 GRPO Improvement of Multi-Turn Success Probability ‣ 3 Methodology ‣ Training Task Reasoning LLM Agents for Multi-turn Task Planning via Single-turn Reinforcement Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Equation</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">19</span></a> is based on Assumption <a href="#S3.Thmassumption1" title="Assumption 3.1 (GRPO improvement on all states). ‣ 3.3 GRPO Improvement of Multi-Turn Success Probability ‣ 3 Methodology ‣ Training Task Reasoning LLM Agents for Multi-turn Task Planning via Single-turn Reinforcement Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.1</span></a>.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS3.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Furthermore, we show that the policy trained with expert trajectories with single-turn GRPO can also generalize to subtask defined below.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.Thmdefinition3" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_definition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S3.Thmdefinition3.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Definition 3.3</span></span><span id="S3.Thmdefinition3.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Subtask and its success probability)<span id="S3.Thmdefinition3.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S3.Thmdefinition3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.Thmdefinition3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.Thmdefinition3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Given the expert trajectory <math id="S3.Thmdefinition3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau^{GT}(T^{GT})=[(s_{0},a_{0}),\dots,(s_{T^{GT}},a_{T^{GT}})]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>τ</mi><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>T</mi><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><msup><mi>T</mi><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow></msup></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><msup><mi>T</mi><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow></msup></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau^{GT}(T^{GT})=[(s_{0},a_{0}),\dots,(s_{T^{GT}},a_{T^{GT}})]</annotation></semantics></math> in <a href="#S2.Thmdefinition2" title="Definition 2.2 (Expert trajectory by stochastic policy). ‣ 2.2 Expert Trajectory with Unique Optimality ‣ 2 Problem Formulation ‣ Training Task Reasoning LLM Agents for Multi-turn Task Planning via Single-turn Reinforcement Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Definition</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.2</span></a> for the full task, a subtask is defined by a completion step <math id="S3.Thmdefinition3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k^{*}&lt;T^{GT}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>k</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo>&lt;</mo><msup><mi>T</mi><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k^{*}&lt;T^{GT}</annotation></semantics></math> where the subtask is completed upon reaching <math id="S3.Thmdefinition3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(s_{k^{*}},a_{k^{*}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><msup><mi>k</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><msup><mi>k</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(s_{k^{*}},a_{k^{*}})</annotation></semantics></math>, i.e., <math id="S3.Thmdefinition3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R_{\text{sub}}(s_{k^{*}},a_{k^{*}})=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>R</mi><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">sub</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><msup><mi>k</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><msup><mi>k</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R_{\text{sub}}(s_{k^{*}},a_{k^{*}})=1</annotation></semantics></math>. The success probability with the fewest turns for subtask at <math id="S3.Thmdefinition3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s_{t},t\leq k^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>≤</mo><msup><mi>k</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s_{t},t\leq k^{*}</annotation></semantics></math> is defined as,</span></p>
<table id="S3.EGx18" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S3.E21"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E21.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle P_{t}^{\text{sub},\pi}(s_{t})=\mathbb{P}_{\pi}\left(R_{\text{sub}}(s_{k^{*}},a_{k^{*}})=1|s_{t}\right)," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>P</mi><mi>t</mi><mrow><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">sub</mtext><mo>,</mo><mi>π</mi></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>ℙ</mi><mi>π</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>R</mi><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">sub</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><msup><mi>k</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><msup><mi>k</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle P_{t}^{\text{sub},\pi}(s_{t})=\mathbb{P}_{\pi}\left(R_{\text{sub}}(s_{k^{*}},a_{k^{*}})=1|s_{t}\right),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(21)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.Thmdefinition3.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.Thmdefinition3.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">which is the probability of achieving subtask goal in the minimal number of steps starting from state <math id="S3.Thmdefinition3.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> at time <math id="S3.Thmdefinition3.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math> under policy <math id="S3.Thmdefinition3.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>π</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS3.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.p5.1" class="ltx_p">Based on the success probability of subtask, we present the following corollary of <a href="#S3.Thmtheorem3" title="Theorem 3.3 (GRPO improves success probability). ‣ 3.3 GRPO Improvement of Multi-Turn Success Probability ‣ 3 Methodology ‣ Training Task Reasoning LLM Agents for Multi-turn Task Planning via Single-turn Reinforcement Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Theorem</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.3</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.Thmcorollary2" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_corollary">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S3.Thmcorollary2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Corollary 3.2</span></span><span id="S3.Thmcorollary2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(GRPO generalization to subtasks)<span id="S3.Thmcorollary2.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S3.Thmcorollary2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.Thmcorollary2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.Thmcorollary2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">For any subtask defined by completion step <math id="S3.Thmcorollary2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k^{*}&lt;T^{GT}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>k</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo>&lt;</mo><msup><mi>T</mi><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k^{*}&lt;T^{GT}</annotation></semantics></math> in <a href="#S3.Thmdefinition3" title="Definition 3.3 (Subtask and its success probability). ‣ 3.3 GRPO Improvement of Multi-Turn Success Probability ‣ 3 Methodology ‣ Training Task Reasoning LLM Agents for Multi-turn Task Planning via Single-turn Reinforcement Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Definition</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.3</span></a>, the GRPO-optimized policy <math id="S3.Thmcorollary2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>π</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi^{*}</annotation></semantics></math> achieves better performance on the subtask than the reference policy <math id="S3.Thmcorollary2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi^{\text{ref}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>π</mi><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">ref</mtext></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi^{\text{ref}}</annotation></semantics></math> for any given state <math id="S3.Thmcorollary2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> at step <math id="S3.Thmcorollary2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t\leq k^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>≤</mo><msup><mi>k</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t\leq k^{*}</annotation></semantics></math>, i.e.,
<math id="S3.Thmcorollary2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{t}^{\text{sub},\pi^{*}}(s_{t})\geq P_{t}^{\text{sub},\pi^{\text{ref}}}(s_{t})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>P</mi><mi>t</mi><mrow><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">sub</mtext><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>π</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>P</mi><mi>t</mi><mrow><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">sub</mtext><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>π</mi><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">ref</mtext></msup></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{t}^{\text{sub},\pi^{*}}(s_{t})\geq P_{t}^{\text{sub},\pi^{\text{ref}}}(s_{t})</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS3.3" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S3.SS3.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.p6.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS3.p6.1.1" class="ltx_text">Since the subtask uses the same expert trajectory prefix as the full task, Assumption <a href="#S3.Thmassumption1" title="Assumption 3.1 (GRPO improvement on all states). ‣ 3.3 GRPO Improvement of Multi-Turn Success Probability ‣ 3 Methodology ‣ Training Task Reasoning LLM Agents for Multi-turn Task Planning via Single-turn Reinforcement Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.1</span></a> also holds for subtask goal with the same single-turn reward function <math id="S3.SS3.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r_{\pi^{GT}}(s,a)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><msup><mi>π</mi><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow></msup></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r_{\pi^{GT}}(s,a)</annotation></semantics></math>. Besides, the recursion condition of <math id="S3.SS3.p6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{t}^{\text{sub},\pi}(s_{t})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>P</mi><mi>t</mi><mrow><mtext>sub</mtext><mo>,</mo><mi>π</mi></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{t}^{\text{sub},\pi}(s_{t})</annotation></semantics></math> also applies. Therefore, following the proof of <a href="#S3.Thmtheorem3" title="Theorem 3.3 (GRPO improves success probability). ‣ 3.3 GRPO Improvement of Multi-Turn Success Probability ‣ 3 Methodology ‣ Training Task Reasoning LLM Agents for Multi-turn Task Planning via Single-turn Reinforcement Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Theorem</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.3</span></a>, by letting <math id="S3.SS3.p6.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T^{GT}=k^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>T</mi><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow></msup><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>k</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T^{GT}=k^{*}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S3.SS3.p6.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>π</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi^{*}</annotation></semantics></math> achieves better success probability than <math id="S3.SS3.p6.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi^{\text{ref}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>π</mi><mtext>ref</mtext></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi^{\text{ref}}</annotation></semantics></math> over <math id="S3.SS3.p6.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{t}^{\text{sub}}(s_{t})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>P</mi><mi>t</mi><mtext>sub</mtext></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{t}^{\text{sub}}(s_{t})</annotation></semantics></math> for any given state <math id="S3.SS3.p6.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> at step <math id="S3.SS3.p6.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t\leq k^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>≤</mo><msup><mi>k</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t\leq k^{*}</annotation></semantics></math>.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S4" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">4 </span>Experiments</h2>

<div id="S4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In the experiments, we aim to answer the following two questions empirically: <span id="S4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">1) How is the performance of multi-turn task planning after GRPO over single-turn task reasoning? 2) Can the LLM agents after GRPO generalize to unseen planning tasks?</span> We answer the first question in <a href="#S4.SS2" title="4.2 Results of Task Planning with Single-turn GRPO ‣ 4 Experiments ‣ Training Task Reasoning LLM Agents for Multi-turn Task Planning via Single-turn Reinforcement Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Section</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.2</span></a> and the second one in <a href="#S4.SS3" title="4.3 Generalization to Unseen Task Planning ‣ 4 Experiments ‣ Training Task Reasoning LLM Agents for Multi-turn Task Planning via Single-turn Reinforcement Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Section</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.3</span></a>, which correspond to <a href="#S3.Thmtheorem3" title="Theorem 3.3 (GRPO improves success probability). ‣ 3.3 GRPO Improvement of Multi-Turn Success Probability ‣ 3 Methodology ‣ Training Task Reasoning LLM Agents for Multi-turn Task Planning via Single-turn Reinforcement Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Theorem</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.3</span></a> and <a href="#S3.Thmcorollary2" title="Corollary 3.2 (GRPO generalization to subtasks). ‣ 3.3 GRPO Improvement of Multi-Turn Success Probability ‣ 3 Methodology ‣ Training Task Reasoning LLM Agents for Multi-turn Task Planning via Single-turn Reinforcement Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Corollary</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.2</span></a>, respectively. Prior to the answers, we first introduce the experimental setup of environment, model training and evaluation metrics.</p>
</div>
<section id="S4.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.1 </span>Experimental Setup</h3>

<section id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Environment and Tasks</h5>

<div id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We evaluate our approach on the <span id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Robotouille</span> benchmark <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib23" title="" class="ltx_ref">23</a>]</cite>, a challenging multi-turn task planning environment designed for testing LLM agents’ reasoning capabilities in cooking scenarios. <span id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Robotouille</span> provides a deterministic long-horizon complex environment where agents must coordinate multiple actions across different kitchen stations to complete recipes. We focus on four progressively challenging cooking tasks with different maximum steps: (1) <span id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Cheese Sandwich</span> - at most 10 steps; (2) <span id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Burger</span> - at most 10 steps; (3) <span id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Cheese Burger</span> - at most 15 steps; and (4) <span id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Double Cheese Burger</span> - at most 23 steps. These tasks were selected to provide increasing complexity in both planning horizon and reasoning requirements, allowing us to evaluate our method’s scalability and generalization across different task difficulties.
While <span id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Robotouille</span> represents a specific domain, its structured nature with consistent state-action distribution under varying complexity levels makes it particularly suitable as a controlled yet comprehensive testbed to empirically validate our theoretical results. Future work will extend it to more diverse agent environments <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib24" title="" class="ltx_ref">24</a>, <a href="#bib.bib25" title="" class="ltx_ref">25</a>, <a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">15</a>]</cite> to demonstrate the broader applicability of single-turn decomposition for multi-turn agent training.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S4.T1" class="ltx_table">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table"><span id="S4.T1.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Table 1</span>: </span><span id="S4.T1.5" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Performance Comparison on Different Tasks. Best results are in <span id="S4.T1.5.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">bold</span>. “—” indicates no successful trajectories.</span></figcaption>
<table id="S4.T1.6" class="ltx_tabular ltx_centering ltx_guessed_headers ltx_align_middle">
<thead class="ltx_thead">
<tr id="S4.T1.6.1" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="S4.T1.6.1.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_border_tt">Results of Cheese Sandwich</th>
<th id="S4.T1.6.1.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_border_tt">SR (<math id="S4.T1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\uparrow" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo stretchy="false">↑</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\uparrow</annotation></semantics></math>)</th>
<th id="S4.T1.6.1.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_border_tt">ASAT (<math id="S4.T1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\downarrow" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo stretchy="false">↓</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\downarrow</annotation></semantics></math>)</th>
<th id="S4.T1.6.1.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_border_tt">ASST (<math id="S4.T1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\downarrow" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo stretchy="false">↓</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\downarrow</annotation></semantics></math>)</th></tr>
</thead>
<tbody class="ltx_tbody">
<tr id="S4.T1.6.2" class="ltx_tr">
