<section id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Expert Trajectory Collection</h5>

<div id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Expert trajectories are collected using rejection sampling from Llama3.3-70B-Instruct <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib21" title="" class="ltx_ref">21</a>]</cite>, which serves as our expert policy <math id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi^{GT}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>π</mi><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi^{GT}</annotation></semantics></math>. To ensure diversity and robustness, we generate expert trajectories across varied kitchen layouts and ingredient placements, creating approximately 100 successful trajectories per task type. Each trajectory <math id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau^{GT}=[(s_{0}^{GT},a_{0}^{GT}),\ldots,(s_{n}^{GT},a_{n}^{GT})]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>τ</mi><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>s</mi><mn>0</mn><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>a</mi><mn>0</mn><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>s</mi><mi>n</mi><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>a</mi><mi>n</mi><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau^{GT}=[(s_{0}^{GT},a_{0}^{GT}),\ldots,(s_{n}^{GT},a_{n}^{GT})]</annotation></semantics></math> satisfies the task completion criteria with <math id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R(s_{n}^{GT},a_{n}^{GT})=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>s</mi><mi>n</mi><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>a</mi><mi>n</mi><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R(s_{n}^{GT},a_{n}^{GT})=1</annotation></semantics></math>. The state-action tokens follow the ReAct agentic framework <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">4</a>]</cite>, where each action <math id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a_{i}^{GT}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>a</mi><mi>i</mi><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a_{i}^{GT}</annotation></semantics></math> consists of structured reasoning followed by a specific action selection from the valid action list indicated from state tokens. This framework enables us to construct single-turn verifiable rewards <math id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.m5" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="r(s_{i}^{GT},\pi_{\theta}(s_{i}^{GT}))=\mathds{1}\{\pi_{\theta}(s_{i}^{GT})=a_{i}^{GT}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>s</mi><mi>i</mi><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>s</mi><mi>i</mi><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>𝟙</mn><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>s</mi><mi>i</mi><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><msubsup><mi>a</mi><mi>i</mi><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r(s_{i}^{GT},\pi_{\theta}(s_{i}^{GT}))=\mathds{1}\{\pi_{\theta}(s_{i}^{GT})=a_{i}^{GT}\}</annotation></semantics></math> for GRPO training, where both the ReAct format and action correctness contribute to the reward by 0.1 and 0.9, respectively.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px3" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Model Training Pipeline</h5>

<div id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Our training pipeline consists of two sequential post-training stages using the VeRL implementation <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib26" title="" class="ltx_ref">26</a>]</cite> of GRPO. First, we perform supervised fine-tuning (SFT) on Qwen2.5-1.5B-Instruct <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib22" title="" class="ltx_ref">22</a>]</cite> using the collected expert trajectories to establish a strong initialization. The SFT stage trains the model to imitate the ReAct pattern for the action tokens.
Subsequently, we apply GRPO with the single-turn verifiable reward function based on all state-action pairs <math id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(s_{i}^{GT},a_{i}^{GT})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>s</mi><mi>i</mi><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>a</mi><mi>i</mi><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(s_{i}^{GT},a_{i}^{GT})</annotation></semantics></math> from expert trajectories.
The SFT takes 8 epochs with batch size of 8 and the GRPO training uses a batch size of 256, learning rate of <math id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1\times 10^{-6}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>6</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1\times 10^{-6}</annotation></semantics></math>, and KL regularization coefficient <math id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta=0.001" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0.001</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta=0.001</annotation></semantics></math> for 50 epochs.</p>
</div>
