</table>
</figure>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px4" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Evaluation Metrics and Baselines</h5>

<div id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We evaluate all models on 10 unseen kitchen layouts per task type, ensuring that the test environments differ from training data in terms of object positions, kitchen topology, and ingredient availability. The timeout lengths of attempt turns are 15, 15, 23, 35 for tasks of <span id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Cheese Sandwich, Burger, Cheese Burger, Double Cheese Burger</span>, respectively.
We assess the model performance of effectiveness and efficiency using three key metrics: (1) <span id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px4.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Success Rate (SR)</span> - the proportion of episodes where the agent successfully completes the task within the step timeout limit; (2) <span id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px4.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Average Steps of All Trajectories (ASAT)</span>
- the mean number of steps taken across all episodes, including timeout failures; and (3) <span id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px4.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Average Steps of Successful Trajectories (ASST)</span>
- the mean number of steps for successfully completed episodes only.
We compare our approach against different-size (1.5B, 3B, 7B, 14B) Qwen2.5 instruct models <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib22" title="" class="ltx_ref">22</a>]</cite> using ReAct prompting <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">4</a>]</cite> and the one right after SFT on expert trajectories but without GRPO training, ablating the contribution of RL for task reasoning.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.2 </span>Results of Task Planning with Single-turn GRPO</h3>

<div id="S4.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><a href="#S4.T1" title="In Environment and Tasks ‣ 4.1 Experimental Setup ‣ 4 Experiments ‣ Training Task Reasoning LLM Agents for Multi-turn Task Planning via Single-turn Reinforcement Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Table</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> presents the results for the four progressively challenging cooking tasks.
Each table compares the proposed method against multiple baselines including different-sized Qwen models and the model without single-turn task reasoning of GRPO. These results provide strong empirical support for <a href="#S3.Thmtheorem3" title="Theorem 3.3 (GRPO improves success probability). ‣ 3.3 GRPO Improvement of Multi-Turn Success Probability ‣ 3 Methodology ‣ Training Task Reasoning LLM Agents for Multi-turn Task Planning via Single-turn Reinforcement Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Theorem</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.3</span></a>, showing that single-turn GRPO training indeed leads to improved multi-turn task planning performance.
Specifically, the results demonstrate the following key findings.</p>
</div>
<section id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Significant Performance Gains by GRPO of Task Reasoning</h5>

<div id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Our model with SFT and GRPO consistently outperforms much larger-size baseline models. Most notably, it achieves 0.70 success rate on the Burger task compared to 0.0 for the 14B baseline, and matches the expert’s 0.70 performance on the Cheese Burger task while being significantly more efficient (15.8 vs 17.4 average steps). The SFT-alone model provides limited success compared to the one followed by GRPO, validating the effectiveness of single-turn RL for task reasoning in <a href="#S3.Thmtheorem3" title="Theorem 3.3 (GRPO improves success probability). ‣ 3.3 GRPO Improvement of Multi-Turn Success Probability ‣ 3 Methodology ‣ Training Task Reasoning LLM Agents for Multi-turn Task Planning via Single-turn Reinforcement Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Theorem</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.3</span></a>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Efficiency and Scalability to Complex Tasks</h5>

<div id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Beyond success rates, our method consistently achieves lower average step counts for successful episodes, indicating more efficient task planning. For instance, on the Cheese Burger task, our method requires only 12.7 steps compared to 15.3 for SFT-only, demonstrating that GRPO learns more optimal policies with minimal steps of task completion in <a href="#S3.Thmdefinition2" title="Definition 3.2 (Success probability with minimal steps). ‣ 3.2 Success Probability for Multi-Turn Task Planning ‣ 3 Methodology ‣ Training Task Reasoning LLM Agents for Multi-turn Task Planning via Single-turn Reinforcement Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Definition</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.2</span></a>. The performance advantage is maintained even on the most challenging Double Cheese Burger task (35-step horizon), where our 1.5B model achieves 0.3 success rate while all larger LLMs fail completely.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.3 </span>Generalization to Unseen Task Planning</h3>

<div id="S4.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">To evaluate the generalizability of LLM agent trained from single-turn task reasoning, we train LLM agents for individual tasks and evaluate their zero-shot performance on all four tasks. Tables <a href="#S4.T2" title="Table 2 ‣ Environment and Tasks ‣ 4.1 Experimental Setup ‣ 4 Experiments ‣ Training Task Reasoning LLM Agents for Multi-turn Task Planning via Single-turn Reinforcement Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>, <a href="#S4.T3" title="Table 3 ‣ Model Training Pipeline ‣ 4.1 Experimental Setup ‣ 4 Experiments ‣ Training Task Reasoning LLM Agents for Multi-turn Task Planning via Single-turn Reinforcement Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>, and <a href="#S4.T4" title="Table 4 ‣ Model Training Pipeline ‣ 4.1 Experimental Setup ‣ 4 Experiments ‣ Training Task Reasoning LLM Agents for Multi-turn Task Planning via Single-turn Reinforcement Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a> present the results under each metric.
These findings provide empirical validation of <a href="#S3.Thmcorollary2" title="Corollary 3.2 (GRPO generalization to subtasks). ‣ 3.3 GRPO Improvement of Multi-Turn Success Probability ‣ 3 Methodology ‣ Training Task Reasoning LLM Agents for Multi-turn Task Planning via Single-turn Reinforcement Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Corollary</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.2</span></a> and demonstrate that our single-turn GRPO approach enables strong cross-task generalization, particularly in the direction from complex to simpler tasks, which is valuable for practical deployment scenarios where a single model needs to handle unseen varying task complexities. Some specific findings are shown below.</p>
</div>
<section id="S4.SS3.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Consistent Generalization of Success Rate over Subtasks</h5>

<div id="S4.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Models trained on more complex tasks demonstrate better generalization to simpler tasks. The agent trained on Double Cheese Burger can solve all tasks with non-zero success rates (0.3 - 0.5), while agents trained on simpler tasks fail on more complex ones. This supports our theoretical result that complex task training captures the necessary subtask planning capabilities in <a href="#S3.Thmcorollary2" title="Corollary 3.2 (GRPO generalization to subtasks). ‣ 3.3 GRPO Improvement of Multi-Turn Success Probability ‣ 3 Methodology ‣ Training Task Reasoning LLM Agents for Multi-turn Task Planning via Single-turn Reinforcement Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Corollary</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.2</span></a>, showing that GRPO training enables generalization to subtasks with shorter horizons. We also observe that success rates generally decrease as the evaluation task becomes more complex relative to the training task, but remain positive when the evaluation task is simpler or of similar complexity.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS3.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Loss of Optimality and Failure on Complex Tasks in Generalization</h5>

<div id="S4.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Even when agents trained on complex tasks successfully generalize to simple tasks, they may require more steps than the agents trained specifically on the target simple tasks. For example, the Double Cheese Burger-trained agent requires 8.2 steps for Burger task completion compared to 8.0 steps for the Burger-trained agent, indicating some loss of efficiency and optimality during generalization. In addition, agents trained on all simpler tasks completely fail on the most complex Double Cheese Burger task, revealing the future directions of the LLM RL post-training community where complex reasoning capabilities are hard to obtain from simpler task reasoning problems.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S5" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">5 </span>Conclusion</h2>

<div id="S5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In this paper, we present a theoretical framework demonstrating that GRPO improvements on single-turn task reasoning result in enhanced multi-turn success probability under minimal steps for challenging multi-turn interaction scenarios. Empirical results validate our approach, with our small-parameter model consistently outperforming larger baselines while requiring fewer steps for task completion. We also show cross-task generalization capabilities both theoretically and empirically.
While limitations remain regarding the reliance on expert trajectories, our work shows how to leverage single-turn RL successes for complex multi-turn task planning, opening promising directions for more capable and efficient LLM-based agents.</p>
</div>
</section>
